※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4918

※ What the compiler can see is an error and what it cannot is a fault, which is
※ the rule arithmetic already follows here.  A condition that comes to a
※ constant falsehood would stop every call of the function, so the program is
※ wrong where it is written rather than where it runs.
※
※ Two types compared is a question the compiler settles, and it keeps that
※ reading inside a clause: a clause over values is what it is, and `⌜u8⌝` there
※ is the type itself rather than a value standing in for one.

fn step(a: u8) → u8 pre(⌜u8⌝ = ⌜u16⌝):
    a

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    ⎕narrow(step(1u8), ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
